    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "The Indian Ocean is shared by many nations whose economic stability depends on safe and open sea lanes. Maritime security challenges therefore require collective regional approaches rather than isolated national responses.\n\nJoint maritime surveillance initiatives, intelligence sharing, and coordinated naval patrols among member states could contribute to maintaining stability in the Bay of Bengal and adjacent waters. Sri Lanka, given its central maritime location, is well positioned to play a leadership role in initiating such cooperation.\n\nUltimately, the incident in the southern seas should be viewed as a strategic warning rather than a single isolated event.\n\nThe Indian Ocean remains one of the world\u2019s most important arteries of commerce, carrying energy supplies, food shipments, and manufactured goods across continents. Ensuring that these waters remain secure is therefore a matter of global importance.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the task ahead is to strengthen maritime resilience through a combination of enhanced surveillance, regional cooperation, and community engagement. By integrating naval capability, economic awareness, and coastal intelligence networks, the country can help ensure that the sea lanes passing its shores remain safe and reliable.\n\nIn an era where geopolitical tensions increasingly extend into the maritime domain, safeguarding the stability of the Indian Ocean is not merely a naval responsibility, it is a national strategic priority.\n\n*Mahil Dole, SSP (Retired), is the former Head of the Counter-Terrorism Division of the State Intelligence Service of Sri Lanka, and has served as Head of the Sri Lankan Delegation at three BIMSTEC Security Conferences. With over 40 years of experience in policing and intelligence, he writes on regional security, interfaith relations, and geopolitical strategy and is the managing director of Smart Security Solutions Pvt\u00a0 Ltd."
