    "chunk_id": "2026-03-07-dailyftlk-fd4cb7f6-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ec415ddcbb889662494164ba22931e23",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1908,
    "chunk_text": "Dipped Products\u2019 50th year  focuses on regional healthcare,  education and community resilience\n\nFrom left: Monaragala District Secretary K. Nishantha, Chief Nursing Officer Upul Rathnayake, Dr. J. Susil Denagama, DPL Managing Director Pushpika Janadheera, General Manager of Logistic and Procurement Sampath Kumara at the\u00a0opening event\n\nManaging Director of DPL Pushpika Janadheera handing over the donations to Dr. J. Susil Denagama and Physiotherapist\n\nAs Dipped Products PLC (DPL) marks its 50th anniversary, the company said it is dedicated to strengthening its ongoing commitment to social development, with targeted interventions in healthcare, education, public services and rural livelihoods.\n\nThe renovation and development of the Physiotherapy Unit at the District General Hospital, Monaragala is the flagship project of the anniversary program. The project included a comprehensive refurbishment of the unit and the donation of a BTL CPMotion rehabilitation machine to support patient recovery and mobility treatment.\n\nStrengthening access to physiotherapy services for patients across the region, this initiative represents a long-term investment in regional healthcare infrastructure, benefiting both patients and medical professionals. The upgraded facility was officially handed over recently by Dipped Products PLC Managing Director Pushpika Janadheera and senior representatives.\n\nAlongside this project, the company has implemented a number of additional community-focused programs.\n\nFollowing the impact of Cyclone Ditwah, DPL extended financial assistance to affected farmer communities in Kegalle, with a focus on families who experienced crop loss and property damage. Based on internal field assessments that considered the severity of impact and livelihood disruption, assistance was distributed among 22 affected farmers to support recovery and help restore their income sources."
  },
