    "chunk_text": "Yet AidData at William & Mary University reports that Chinese state institutions have invested in 363 ports worldwide over the past 25 years, totaling US$23.9 billion. Nearly half of this portfolio went to high-income countries, including Australia, New Zealand, Spain, Brunei, and Singapore, reflecting an effort to secure trade networks and mitigate East-West supply chain decoupling. Even so, setbacks such as Panama reveal the limits of influence, illustrating that infrastructure alone cannot fully insulate China from geopolitical realities.\n\nPresident Donald Trump is expected to visit China in April amid ongoing trade disputes; while the Supreme Court ruled that many of his broad emergencybased tariffs exceeded presidential authority, his administration responded by imposing new global levies under alternative statutory powers, continuing to maintain high duties on Chinese exports. Trump\u2019s rhetoric continues to be unpredictable, alternating between combative and conciliatory, while US military operations in the Indo-Pacific, including maritime deployments near the South China Sea, apply strategic pressure on Beijing.\n\nA subtle but transformative development noted during this year\u2019s Two Sessions is the repatriation of Chinese experts from Western institutions. Senior scientists, engineers, and economists who trained or worked in the United States, Europe, and Canada are increasingly returning to China, bringing expertise in artificial intelligence, biotechnology, and advanced manufacturing. These returnees are pivotal in accelerating China\u2019s technological self-reliance, ensuring that innovation is not merely imported but indigenously adapted and scaled."
