    "chunk_text": "The Two Sessions have laid out clear economic, social, and technological objectives for 2026. According to the government work report submitted to the legislature, China sets a growth target of 4.5 to 5 per cent for the year, alongside the creation of over 12 million urban jobs. Consumer price growth is projected at around two per cent, the deficit-to-GDP ratio at roughly four per cent, and personal income is expected to rise in step with overall growth. Grain output is targeted at 700 million metric tonnes, and carbon dioxide emissions per unit of GDP are to fall by approximately 3.8 per cent. Policymakers emphasize the expansion of elderly care services and the development of the silver economy in response to an ageing population.\n\nThe PLA is expected to achieve significant advancements in information warfare, mechanization, and operational projection by 2027. However, the military purge has injected uncertainty into the synchronization of technological upgrades with operational readiness. Vacancies at the highest levels, coupled with less experienced replacements, could impede rapid decision-making in crises, raising questions about the timing and feasibility of Beijing\u2019s more ambitious defence objectives.\n\nChina\u2019s global ambitions are further tested by infrastructure setbacks and Belt and Road uncertainties. Despite significant overseas investment in ports and related logistics, the Panama Canal incident highlights the limits of influence. Simultaneously, domestic policy goals\u2014expanding consumer spending, supporting an aging population, and advancing science and technology\u2014reflect an attempt to balance internal development with external projection. The Two Sessions thus serve as a nexus for domestic and international strategy, combining economic planning, military reform, technological advancement, and diplomatic positioning. Still waters run deep.\n\nKebiliththa forest slowly recovering from 2021 devastation, but cattl