    "md5": "b7ccac30ec5c566dbda4e03fabb6068a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1833,
    "chunk_text": "Kebiliththa forest slowly recovering from 2021 devastation, but cattle grazing emerging as new threat\n\nThe scarred forests of Kebiliththa in Sri Lanka\u2019s Monaragala District are slowly showing signs of recovery nearly five years after large-scale illegal clearing shocked conservationists and triggered national outrage.\n\nEnvironmentalists who recently visited the area say that although vegetation has begun to regenerate naturally, the forest ecosystem still bears the deep wounds of the destruction that took place in 2021. More worryingly, they warn that illegal cattle grazing has now emerged as a major threat to the fragile regeneration of the forest.\n\nEnvironmental scientist Hemantha Withanage said a recent field inspection revealed that the forest, which once suffered widespread clearing, is slowly attempting to heal.\n\n\u201cWe went to the Kebiliththa forest recently to observe the present condition,\u201d Withanage said. \u201cWhen the destruction was halted in 2021, around 8,000 trees \u2014 locally referred to as \u2018Yakkha Puththu\u2019 \u2014 had already been cut down. Today we can see that some natural regeneration has begun. But the forest is still very vulnerable.\u201d\n\nThe Kebiliththa forest complex lies within the dry-zone wilderness landscape of southeastern Sri Lanka and forms part of a vital ecological corridor that connects several wildlife habitats. The area supports elephants, leopards, reptiles, birds and numerous endemic species that depend on intact forest ecosystems.\n\nThe forest gained national attention in 2021 when environmental groups exposed extensive illegal clearing carried out in parts of the reserve. Large areas had been cut down primarily for cultivation and land encroachment, sparking protests from conservationists who warned that the destruction would have long-term ecological consequences."
  },
