    "language": "en",
    "md5": "10ac372c2202d77fd464ce5ac93d9e30",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1782,
    "chunk_text": "He was treated with honours by the British government, and had a meeting with Governor Havelock, in Kandy where the Governor had gone to arrange for a ball in celebration of Queen Victoria\u2019s 77th birthday. But of course the main purpose of his visit to Kandy was to visit the Temple of the Tooth, though he was not impressed by the temple, which was crowded, nor by the Tooth, which he was allowed to view.\n\nI had had no idea previously that the Thai monarchy had been so keen on the Temple of the Tooth, though this made sense, given that the Siyam Nikaya had been started with support from Thailand. And there was another Thai prince, which prompts a fascinating account by Kamalakaran of him being sent for military training in Russia, and marrying a Russian lady, though that marriage ended in divorce. In the first account in the book, the glimpse offered of the relations between the king and his son was delightful.\n\nThat account is preceded as noted by an account of how Colombo developed, and its burgeoning reputation as an attractive destination for tourists. Kamalakaran has obviously done a great deal of research, and this introduction \u2013 as indeed the rest of the book \u2013 is full of quotations from the writings of visitors. These include not only tourists, but also journalists, who celebrate the place, beginning with a 1851 article in a South Carolina paper.\n\nThe introduction also has a section on the arrival of the missionaries, a practice begun by Americans, though turned on its head with the mission to revive Buddhism of Colonel Olcott. And there is mention of the Salvation Army, which returns again and again in the descriptions of visitors in the rest of the book, their marching and the fact that they dressed, even the women, in Ceylonese costumes."
  },
