    "md5": "4ed92645f5b278bd375596c6d58d0d97",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1619,
    "chunk_text": "The introduction also has a section on the arrival of the missionaries, a practice begun by Americans, though turned on its head with the mission to revive Buddhism of Colonel Olcott. And there is mention of the Salvation Army, which returns again and again in the descriptions of visitors in the rest of the book, their marching and the fact that they dressed, even the women, in Ceylonese costumes.\n\nAmongst the well known visitors Kamalakaran talks about are Don Bradman, the future Emperor Hirohito, Arthur Conan Doyle, Mark Twain, Mahatma Gandhi, Orabi Pasha and Anton Chekhov. I have mentioned these in the order in which they occur, which highlights a weakness of the book, since putting the visitors in chronological order instead of zigzagging across the centuries would have given a better idea of changes that had occurred during the nearly 70 years the book covers.\n\nOrabi Pasha was not really a visitor, for he had been sent into exile here by the British from Egypt, after a rebellion against their creature the Khedive whose Prime Minister he had been. Understandably enough, having been forced to spend 18 years in Ceylon, he was far less enthusiastic about the place than others showcased in the book. He found the humidity insupportable and after nine long years he was moved to Kandy, where he got better.\n\nHe came with his family, but then had children by \u2018Sinhalese wives\u2019 but there is no trace now of such progeny. What has lasted is Zahira College, which he inspired leading Muslims to found, at a time when Buddhists and Hindus were also developing schools to counteract Christian influence."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
