    "md5": "843961e112b13a9fea8f1f76c674042e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "He came with his family, but then had children by \u2018Sinhalese wives\u2019 but there is no trace now of such progeny. What has lasted is Zahira College, which he inspired leading Muslims to found, at a time when Buddhists and Hindus were also developing schools to counteract Christian influence.\n\nThere are accounts of lesser known travellers as well, though I suppose the philanthropist Andrew Carnegies is quite famous. And the prolific Belgian traveller Jules Leclercq and the Russian philosopher Nicholas Roerich are not unknown. But there are also two obscure American ladies, Mary Thorn Carpenter who travelled on her own while in her twenties, and the even younger Jane Sherman, a dancer.\n\nSherman\u2019s trip is the most unusual of those in the book, for she was on a tour of Asia with an obscure dance troupe. The trip had been exhausting, for they were on a shoestring budget, but Sherman was not at all well when she got to Colombo so that she could not perform, which meant as Karunakaran puts it that she had a respite while here, and thoroughly enjoyed her stay.\n\nThere is very little of Karunakaran himself in the book, though the introduction makes it clear that he has thoroughly studied his subject, and applied himself to the city, the port, and the various reactions of the travellers he has selected with both dedication and affection.\n\nFor the most part he is content to let his characters speak for themselves, and has selected extracts from diaries that bring out their individuality. As we saw with the Thai prince, he notes the affection between father and son, and he deals with a similar subject in describing the worries Mrs Bradman felt about her husband. Oddly enough, what emerges most strongly from the account of Bradman is how sick he was so often, on the ship when he first came over, and in Ceylon too, so that once he was unable to take the field, to the chagrin of his enthusiastic supporters in Colombo."
  },
