    "chunk_text": "Fortunately, with the support from the International Monetary Fund and friendly nations, Sri Lanka began to recover from the crises slowly but steadily. The recovery from a crisis is a long process, and as the IMF representative in Sri Lanka, Martha T Woldemichael, had correctly pronounced in a recent public address, it is \u2018not a finished story\u2019 yet [2]. She had highlighted the need for sustained reforms, fiscal credibility, and policy consistency to translate the emerging poor macroeconomic stability into lasting improvements in living standards and to build investor confidence. What this means is that the economy is not yet off the hook and Sri Lankans should continue the painful suffering for some more time until the crisis levels are brought to a control.\n\nA prolonged war in the Mideast that is also dragging other nations into the fray is not a welcome sign for the global economy as highlighted by the IMF\u2019s Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva in a recent international conference held in Bangkok [5]. She said that if the war is prolonged, it will obviously deliver a global economic shock by increasing energy prices, downgrading market sentiments, hurting global economic growth, and raising presently subdued inflation levels, thereby making new demands on policymakers. If these adversities are placed in terms of severity on a line, Sri Lanka\u2019s wounded economy stands at the farthest end."
