    "md5": "4830c7874d5abc40164c36865f53ef88",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1520,
    "chunk_text": "Women & Entrepreneurship\n\nAlthough the word \u201cwoman\u201d has historically been associated with domestic responsibilities, family security, and social relationships, in the modern era women have moved beyond these traditional boundaries and become a powerful force for economic development. Today, female leadership in entrepreneurship is not only a reflection of social change but also a direct contribution to the economic progress of a country. The concept of \u201cWomen and Entrepreneurship\u201d has become an important discussion in society as women transform economic activities through creativity, sensitivity, and long-term vision.\n\nEntrepreneurship is a journey that combines risk-taking, innovation, resource management, and the identification of market opportunities. Women bring unique strengths to this field, particularly their ability to recognize social needs and manage multiple responsibilities effectively. By balancing both family life and professional life, women demonstrate that leadership is determined not by gender but by capability, dedication, and commitment.\n\nIn developing countries such as Sri Lanka, female entrepreneurship plays a particularly important role. Women are increasingly participating in rural small and medium enterprises (SMEs) and making significant contributions to sectors such as food processing, handicrafts, agribusiness, tourism services, and digital businesses. These ventures not only increase household income but also stimulate local economic activity and development."
  },
