    "md5": "e6c47b466e3367ec27839ec340b04349",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1783,
    "chunk_text": "In developing countries such as Sri Lanka, female entrepreneurship plays a particularly important role. Women are increasingly participating in rural small and medium enterprises (SMEs) and making significant contributions to sectors such as food processing, handicrafts, agribusiness, tourism services, and digital businesses. These ventures not only increase household income but also stimulate local economic activity and development.\n\nAt the same time, digital transformation has created new opportunities for women entrepreneurs. Through social media marketing, e-commerce platforms, and online payment systems, small businesses can now reach not only local markets but also international customers. A home-based business, when supported with effective digital marketing strategies, can grow into a global enterprise. This has introduced a modern economic model that allows women to generate income without being limited by time or location.\n\nEntrepreneurship also serves as a powerful tool for social empowerment. When women achieve financial independence, the overall well-being of their families improves. Children gain better educational opportunities, and women gain stronger decision-making power within their households and communities. In the long term, this contributes to social equality and economic stability.\n\nTherefore, it is important to create a supportive and sustainable environment for women entrepreneurs. Business training programs, mentorship networks, microfinance facilities, and incubation centers can provide essential guidance and support. In addition, promoting entrepreneurship education in universities and vocational institutions will help cultivate an entrepreneurial mindset among young women and prepare the next generation of business leaders."
  },
