    "chunk_text": "\u201cWays of Looking\u201d \u2013 A lecture on Martin Wickramasinghe at Sapumal Foundation\n\n\u201cWays of Looking: Martin Wickramasinghe and the Currents of Sri Lankan Art\u201d, a lecture on Martin Wickramasinghe\u2019s writings on art and culture, will unveil at The Garden Gallery at the Sapumal Foundation on Tuesday, 31 March 2026 from 04.45 pm.\n\nOrganized by the Martin Wickramasinghe Trust, the lecture focuses on Wickramasinghe\u2019s evolving views on art history in the context of early 20th century Sri Lanka and South Asia. It will be presented by Uditha Devapriya. Opening remarks will be given by Avishka Mario Senewiratne, while closing remarks will be given by Hasini Haputhanthri. The session will be moderated by Samindi Warnasooriya of the Open University.\n\nThough not himself an artist, Wickramasinghe commented on art, including on painting. During his most formative years, a number of scholars explored these aspects of Sri Lankan and South Asian society. Ananda Coomaraswamy\u2019s Mediaeval Sinhalese Art was published just two years after Wickramasinghe came to Colombo. In Colombo, he came across the work of scholars like E. B. Havell and Alfred Foucher. He reflected on their writings and formed his views on the subjects they explored. By the end of his life, his reputation as a cultural critic had grown so much that no fewer than six modern artists \u2013 including Harry Pieris, Richard Gabriel, and David Paynter \u2013 had drawn him.\n\nThe lecture at the Sapumal Foundation traces his interest in art to his early life in Koggala and his immersion in art history. It assesses his critiques of scholars of Sri Lankan and South Asian art. Finally, it appraises his reputation as a leading literary figure against the backdrop of cultural modernism in Sri Lanka and in South Asia."
