    "description": "Are We Failing Our Youth? The Reality Behind Sri Lankan\u2019s Job Crisis",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.colombotelegraph.com/index.php/are-we-failing-our-youth-the-reality-behind-sri-lankans-job-crisis/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "colombotelegraphcom",
    "time_ut": 1773546809.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-03-15-colombotelegraphcom-d0e07d3f-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "00a3b61af46dcfdc2b3871c6d8e4c1db",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1385,
    "chunk_text": "Today, it is becoming extremely hard for many young Sri Lankans to obtain jobs or Secure job placement. Even when opportunities come their way, often much qualified graduates find that they are working outside of their qualification. A Marketing graduate may have to find employment in any other field, while an IT student of higher skills faces difficulties in getting real-time exposure in the practical world. Meanwhile, in various sectors, the salary for freshers is at lower level, and it is difficult for young employees to cover their expenses, support their families, and save money. This is compounded by the lack of jobs. For the past years, the government and private sector have failed to create employment to match the number of fresh graduates joining the job market. On other hand, the unstable state of the economy, coupled with high inflation and currency value fluctuations, has affected businesses in creating job opportunities.\n\nEventually, the crossroads the Sri Lankan youth face today is an invitation to redefine the future of work. While the traditional paths of employment may be narrowing, the potential for self-driven innovation is Extensive. By embracing the risks and rewards of entrepreneurship, the current generation can move beyond the shadow of job insecurity and become the architects of a more resilient, self-sufficient, and innovative Sri Lanka."
  },
