    "chunk_size_bytes": 1629,
    "chunk_text": "Heavy entertainment \u2026 by way of rockets and missiles!\n\nThe war is taking its toll on the entertainment scene, as well, especially in the Gulf States.\n\nRight now, hardly any night spots are active and that is because of Ramadan.\n\n\u201cAll our music shows are stopped for this month, in view of Ramadan. Yes, there was also an attack here, but we are staying safe,\u201d said Niluk Uswaththa, a Sri Lankan musician, with the Seven Notes band, based in Dubai.\n\nIn Doha. I was told, by another Sri Lankan musician, Tiron Ismet, that there is heavy entertainment \u2026 by way of rockets and missiles by Iran!\n\nNo doubt, entertainment in the Gulf States will be experiencing disruptions, due to the ongoing conflict, until normalcy returns.\n\nMajor events, concerts, and shows across the region are facing disruptions.\n\nWhile there\u2019s no explicit confirmation that entertainment is at a standstill, in the Gulf region, it\u2019s possible that some events and attractions will be affected, with cancellations and postponements likely.\n\nArtistes like American singer, songwriter and rapper Kesha, and world-renowned Belgian DJ Charlotte de Witte have cancelled performances due to travel-related issues and airspace closures.\n\nHowever, according to reports coming my way, some nightspots in the Gulf region are likely to operate, after the Ramadan, with adjustments to their programmes.\n\nThe silent crisis: A humanitarian plea for Sri Lankan healthcare\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
